\begin_layout Chapter*
Preface to the Fall 2026 Edition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These notes provide a rigorous first course in graduate microeconomic theory.
 The Fall 2026 edition preserves the substantive coverage of the Fall 2024 course while improving the exposition,
 notation,
 organization,
 and portability of the source files.
 The central discipline throughout is to distinguish primitives,
 behavioral assumptions,
 optimization problems,
 equilibrium conditions,
 and empirical implications.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The material is cumulative.
 Part I develops the mathematical language of binary relations and connects preferences to observable choice.
 Part II derives individual demand and extends the benchmark model to limited attention and uncertainty.
 Part III studies aggregation and stochastic choice.
 Part IV develops production,
 competitive markets,
 and general equilibrium.
 Part V treats equilibrium restrictions,
 trade under uncertainty,
 matching,
 and computable general equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter*
Notation and Conventions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following conventions apply unless a chapter explicitly states otherwise.
 They are collected here so that the same symbol does not silently change meaning across topics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
There are 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 commodities,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell=1,\ldots,L$
\end_inset

.
 Commodity bundles are column vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in X\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Consumers are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,I$
\end_inset

;
 firms are indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $j=1,\ldots,J$
\end_inset

.
 Consumption is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 production by 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

,
 and endowments by 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Prices are column vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset
, unless a result explicitly permits zero boundary prices.
 Set difference is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $A\setminus B$
\end_inset
.
 The operators 
\begin_inset Formula $\max$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\min$
\end_inset
 are typeset as mathematical operators.
 The scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 denotes wealth.
 The budget set is 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Marshallian demand is 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 Hicksian demand is 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

,
 indirect utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 and the expenditure function is 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The dot 
\begin_inset Formula $\cdot$
\end_inset

 denotes the Euclidean inner product,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 denotes transpose.
 A prime marks an alternative object,
 as in 
\begin_inset Formula $p'$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $x'$
\end_inset

;
 it is not used for transpose.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Weak preference,
 strict preference,
 and indifference are denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

,
 respectively.
 Choice rules are denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 only when the surrounding section defines the domain and whether the rule is single- or set-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For a differentiable vector-valued function,
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x$
\end_inset

 is its Jacobian with respect to prices,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u$
\end_inset

 is the column gradient of a scalar-valued function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part
Foundations of Choice and Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Basics of Consumer Theory
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Preliminaries
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consumer theory begins with feasible choice and observable demand.
 This chapter introduces the commodity space,
 budget sets,
 demand,
 Walras' law,
 homogeneity of degree zero,
 the weak axiom of revealed preference,
 and the Slutsky matrix.
 The next chapter develops the abstract connection between choice and preference;
 the utility-maximization chapter then joins these two perspectives.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Environment:
 Commodities and Budget Set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First we describe formally the goods and services that a consumer may consume.
 We call the object of consumption 
\series bold
commodity.
 
\series default
We assume that the
\series bold
 commodity space
\series default
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 where 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq1$
\end_inset

 is the number of commodities.
 We refer to any commodity by 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\cdots,L\}$
\end_inset

 and to the quantity of the commodity by 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{x}_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
consumption bundle or commodity bundle 
\series default
is given by the vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{x}=(\overline{x}_{1},\ldots,\overline{x}_{L})^{\top}$
\end_inset
.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the commodity space may be too generic for some modeling purposes (e.g.,
 the situation at hand may rule out extremely large quantities of some commodities,
 or negative quantities are impossible) we use from now on the 
\series bold
consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 
\series default
Remember that the consumption set is an abstract concept that can be defined according to each modeling situation,
 but from now on we will let 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In real life,
 the consumer cannot consume anything he likes from the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 He is constrained either by his available wealth or by other reasons.
 Here we focus on cases where the only constraint is wealth.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 be the consumer's wealth (broadly defined).
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be the vector of commodity prices.
 The consumer is then limited to choose from his budget set.
 The budget set is a subset of the choice set 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)\subseteq X$
\end_inset

 and it is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)=\{x\in X:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Demand Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 After specifying the consumer environment,
 we identify the 
\series bold
primitive 
\series default
 to be modeled:
 the demand function.
 The demand function is a mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $x:\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}\to X$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $((p,w)\to\overline{x})$
\end_inset

 that takes as inputs price-wealth pairs and produces a commodity bundle such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)\leq w$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 denotes the quantity of commodity 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 consumed at prices and wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Observe that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is an empirical object,
 we have not explained yet how it came to be.
 Also note that we have made a very strong assumption when taking a function as our primitive.
Observing a demand function would require observing the consumer's choice at every price--wealth pair,
 which is impossible in practice.
 We therefore use finite observations and testable restrictions throughout the book.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Cobb-Douglas Demand Function.
 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)=(\frac{\alpha w}{p_{1}},\frac{(1-\alpha)w}{p_{2}})^{\top}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Consistency in Consumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With the environment and demand primitive in place,
 we turn to consistency in consumption choices.
 This approach restricts observable behavior before imposing a specific preference representation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We will impose a set of restrictions or axioms over the Marshallian demand function that we expect to hold in all situations irrespective of the details.
 Here we understand modeling consumption as restricting the set of allowable behaviors.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These axioms are minimal theoretical requirements,
 not empirical laws that every dataset must satisfy.
 Their value is that they make the set of admissible behavior explicit and provide a baseline against which richer models can be compared.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Walras' law) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to satisfy Walras' law if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

 for all price-wealth pairs.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Walras' law imposes budget exhaustion;
 it rules out choices that leave unspent wealth when a locally nonsatiated consumer could purchase more.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(No money illusion/Homogeneity of degree zero (HD0)) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to satisfy Homogeneity of Degree Zero if and only if for any 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\alpha p,\alpha w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
HD0 says that multiplying all prices and wealth by the same positive scalar does not change demand.
 It is a normalization of units,
 not a behavioral claim about intelligence.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Axiom
(Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference -WARP) A demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is said to satisfy the WARP if and only if for any two distinct price-wealth pairs 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\neq x(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

 it follows that if 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{*},w^{*})\leq w$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The WARP is the most interesting of the restrictions presented until this point.
 It captures the idea that there is stability in the choices that consumers make.
 Notice the 'revealed preference' in the name of the WARP,
 it comes from the idea that by observing demand behavior underlying consumer preferences are revealed to the observer.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's define a revealed preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}$
\end_inset

 that stands for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

directly revealed preferred to
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 this is a binary relation on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

) defined as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{1},x^{1})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{2},x^{2})$
\end_inset

 be observed consumption bundles with its corresponding price,
 then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{1}R^{D}x^{2}\iff (p^{1})^{\top}x^{1}\geq (p^{1})^{\top}x^{2}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that the hypothesis in the WARP says 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p^{*},w^{*})\leq w$
\end_inset

,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $y=x(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

 and recall that by Walras' law 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

,
 also let 
\begin_inset Formula $z=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 This means that 
\begin_inset Formula $w=p\cdot z\geq p\cdot y$
\end_inset

 which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $zR^{D}y$
\end_inset

,
 or in words,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 is directly revealed preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
 The WARP is then the requirement that if 
\begin_inset Formula $zR^{D}y$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 cannot be strictly directly revealed preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
 This means that we do not allow 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot z\leq p^{*}\cdot y$
\end_inset

 or equivalently it must be the case that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)>w^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}$
\end_inset

 is declared to satisfy the weak axiom.
 Recall that this relation is not necessarily representing actual tastes/preferences,
 we do not need them to do that.
 We are only establishing a condition that for many economists (back in the time of Samuelson) was the minimal requirement that all consumers fulfilled.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Comparative Statics and Slutsky Matrix
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After establishing the axioms of behavior,
 the next step is to study their implications for the parameters of the model.
 The parameters of the model in our study of consumer behavior are prices and wealth pairs.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The price Jacobian is 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)\in\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}$
\end_inset

;
 its 
\begin_inset Formula $(\ell,k)$
\end_inset

 entry is 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{p_{k}}x_{\ell}(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{w}x(p,w)=(\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w))_{\ell=1}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be the vector of wealth effects.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The Slutsky matrix of a demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

 or the total change of demand with respect to a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Slutsky compensated variation of prices
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 A Slutsky-compensated price variation leaves the previously chosen bundle affordable at the new prices.
 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{\ell k}=\partial_{p_{k}}x_{\ell}(p,w)+\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)x_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Fix a reference bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

 and consider the compensated demand 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,p\cdot q)$
\end_inset

.
 The chain rule gives 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}[x(p,p\cdot q)]=D_{p}x(p,p\cdot q)+D_{w}x(p,p\cdot q)q^{\top}$
\end_inset

,
 when 
\begin_inset Formula $q=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and Walras' law gives 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot q=w$
\end_inset

, this derivative is the Slutsky matrix:
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of Walras' Law for the Slutsky Matrix
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Fact
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfy Walras' law then:
 (i) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}D_{w}x(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

,
 (ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}D_{p}x(p,w)=-x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

,
 (iii) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\top}S(p,w)=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The previous fact part (i) implies that there exists at least one normal good,
 the reason is that there has to be at least one 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{w}x_{l}(p,w)>0$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{\ell}p_{\ell}\partial_{w}x_{\ell}(p,w)=1$
\end_inset

.
 In general,
 Walras' law restricts the wealth effects,
 while part (iii) restricts consumption changes under Slutsky-compensated price changes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of Homogeneity of Degree Zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Claim
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfy Walras' law and homogeneity of degree zero then 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By HD0 we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $x(\lambda p,\lambda w)=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0$
\end_inset

,
 we derive by 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 and obtain.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(\lambda p,\lambda w)p+D_{w}x(\lambda p,\lambda w)w=0$
\end_inset

.
 We let 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=1$
\end_inset

,
 and recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x(p,w)=w$
\end_inset

 by Walras' law then:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}x(p,w)p+D_{w}x(p,w)p\cdot x=S(p,w)p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Implications of WARP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main implication of the WARP while assuming Walras' law is the Compensated Law of Demand (CLD).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
(CLD) We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the Compensated Law of Demand if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*}-p)\cdot(x(p^{*},w^{*})-x(p,w))<0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p^{*},w^{*})\neq x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w^{*}=p^{*}\cdot x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We next establish that WARP implies the compensated law of demand.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfy Walras' law and WARP. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies the CLD.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y=x(p^{*},w^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By the assumption that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}x(p,w)=w^{*}$
\end_inset

 and by Walras' law we have that:
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot z=w$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot z=w^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Also we have 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot y=w^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot(y-z)=0$
\end_inset

.
 By WARP since 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot z\leq w^{*}$
\end_inset

 it must be the case that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y>w$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have then 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot y-p\cdot z>0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot(y-z)>0$
\end_inset

 subtract this from that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\cdot(y-z)=0$
\end_inset

 to obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{*}-p)'(y-z)<0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $y\neq z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The differential version yields the following corollary.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Corollary
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and WARP. Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is such that the symmetric part of the Slutsky matrix,
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

, is negative semidefinite: for every 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and every price-wealth pair,
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\mathsf T}S(p,w)a\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law and WARP. Then,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
(p^{*}-p)\cdot[x(p^{*},p^{*}\cdot x(p,w))-x(p,w)]\leq0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I consider a case such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $dp=(p^{*}-p)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The differential version of the CLD is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dp\cdot dx\leq0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I compute 
\begin_inset Formula $dx=D_{p}x(p,w)dp+D_{w}x(p,w)dw$
\end_inset

,
 notice that by Slutsky compensation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dw=x(p,w)\cdot dp=dp\cdot x(p,w).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dp\cdot dx=dp^{\top}[D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}]dp,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
dp\cdot dx=dp^{\top}S(p,w)dp\leq0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

