end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout









\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Marshallian and compensated price effects
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Slutsky decomposition separates the total response of demand to a price change from the response at fixed purchasing power.  For a differentiable demand function,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\partial_{p_k}x_\ell(p,w)=S_{\ell k}(p,w)-x_k(p,w)\,\partial_w x_\ell(p,w).
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under WARP and Walras' law the compensated matrix has a negative-semidefinite symmetric part.  Thus a positive Marshallian own-price derivative \begin_inset Formula $\partial_{p_\ell}x_\ell>0$\end_inset
 is possible only when the income effect is sufficiently adverse: the good is inferior (\begin_inset Formula $\partial_w x_\ell<0$\end_inset
), and the income effect dominates the nonpositive compensated effect.  This is the Giffen mechanism.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The possibility of a Giffen response does not by itself establish a utility representation.  To derive one from data, impose and test the full integrability conditions developed later: budget exhaustion, homogeneity, WARP (or the stronger GARP on finite data), and the relevant regularity assumptions.  The compensated response, rather than the Marshallian derivative alone, is the object restricted by revealed preference.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Preference and Choice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
From Choice Data to Preference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Choice data,
 preference relations,
 and utility representations are three descriptions of the same decision problem.
 This chapter fixes the logical relations among them before prices and wealth are introduced in the consumer-theory chapter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Choice environments
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 be a nonempty set of alternatives and let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}\subseteq2^{X}\setminus\{\emptyset\}$
\end_inset

 be the family of feasible menus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Choice correspondence.
 A choice correspondence is a map 
\begin_inset Formula $c:\mathcal{A}\rightrightarrows X$
\end_inset

 satisfying 
\begin_inset Formula $\emptyset\neq c(A)\subseteq A$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 A choice structure is the pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Rationalization.
 A binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 rationalizes 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $c(A)=\{x\in A:x\succeq y\text{ for every }y\in A\}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 It weakly rationalizes the data if the equality is replaced by inclusion.
 A rational preference relation is complete and transitive.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Preference relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
For a relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

,
 define its strict and symmetric parts by 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ y\iff x\succeq y\text{ and not }y\succeq x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x\sim y\iff x\succeq y\text{ and }y\succeq x$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is complete and transitive,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\succ$
\end_inset

 is irreflexive and transitive,
 while 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation.
 These elementary implications are the order-theoretic basis for utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Finite utility representation.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is finite,
 a preference relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 has a real-valued utility representation if and only if it is complete and transitive.
 A representation is unique up to a strictly increasing transformation of its range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For an infinite commodity or choice space,
 representation requires additional assumptions such as continuity and a suitable countability condition.
 The continuous-utility theorem used later is stated in the consumer-theory chapter.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Revealed preference and WARP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Direct revealed preference.
 For a choice structure 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathcal{A},c)$
\end_inset

,
 write 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq^{D}y$
\end_inset

 if there is a menu 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in c(A)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

.
 Write 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succ^{D}y$
\end_inset

 if, in addition, 
\begin_inset Formula $y\notin c(A)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{*}$
\end_inset

 be the reflexive-transitive closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq^{D}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Weak Axiom of Revealed Preference (WARP).
 The data satisfy WARP when 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq^{D}y$
\end_inset

 implies not 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ^{D}x$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently,
 if two menus contain both 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

,
 choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 from one and strictly choosing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 from the other is ruled out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Necessity of WARP.
 If a complete and transitive preference relation rationalizes a choice structure,
 then the structure satisfies WARP.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in c(A)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in A$
\end_inset

,
 rationalization gives 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset

.
 If a second menu strictly chose 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 it would give 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$
\end_inset

,
 contradicting the first comparison.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
GARP on finite data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Generalized Axiom of Revealed Preference (GARP).
 A finite dataset satisfies GARP when 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq^{*}y$
\end_inset

 never occurs together with 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succ^{D}x$
\end_inset

.
 Thus an indirect revealed-preference chain may return to an alternative only when the return comparison is not strict.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
Finite-data rationalizability (cited).
For a finite budget dataset with positive prices and observed expenditure exhaustion,
 GARP is equivalent to weak rationalizability by a complete and transitive preference relation on the observed budgets.
 Afriat's theorem strengthens this conclusion to a locally nonsatiated,
 continuous and concave utility representation.
 The proof is deferred to the Afriat section of the consumer-theory chapter,
 where the finite inequalities are written explicitly.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
WARP is the one-step restriction;
 GARP is its transitive closure and is therefore the relevant condition when the data do not contain every pair of alternatives in a common menu.
 Exact single-valued rationalization can require additional tie-breaking assumptions,
 so the theorem above is stated for weak rationalization.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\{a,b,c\}$
\end_inset

 and suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $a\succ^{D}b$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b\succ^{D}c$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c\succ^{D}a$
\end_inset

.
 This strict cycle violates GARP and cannot be generated by a transitive preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The separation between the abstract choice language and the budget-set language is deliberate.
 In the next chapter,
 prices and wealth impose additional structure,
 and the Afriat inequalities provide a finite certificate that can be checked mechanically.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Consumer Theory:
 Utility Maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This chapter derives demand from preference maximization and develops the duality between utility maximization and expenditure minimization.
 Throughout,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $L\geq1$
\end_inset

 is the fixed number of commodities,
 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $W=\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Demand Correspondences
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A Walrasian demand correspondence is a map
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x:P\times W\rightrightarrows X
\]

\end_inset

with nonempty values such that every 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot q\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 If demand is generated by a choice correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x=c\circ B$
\end_inset

, where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B(p,w)=\{q\in X:p\cdot q\leq w\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
With two perfect-substitute goods and utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u(q)=q_{1}+q_{2}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w)=\begin{cases}
\{(w/p_{1},0)\}, & p_{1}<p_{2},\\
\{(0,w/p_{2})\}, & p_{2}<p_{1},\\
\{q\in\mathbb{R}^{2}_{+}:p_{1}q_{1}+p_{2}q_{2}=w\}, & p_{1}=p_{2}.
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The equal-price value is the entire budget line, not a pair of independent intervals.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For later use, distinguish the following properties.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A correspondence has a closed graph if 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{n},w^{n})\to(p,w)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{n}\in x(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{n}\to q$
\end_inset

 imply 
\begin_inset Formula $q\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Closed graph and upper hemicontinuity are not equivalent without additional compactness or local-boundedness conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is compact valued if 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is compact for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 In Euclidean space, compactness is equivalent to being closed and bounded.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It is convex valued if 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha q+(1-\alpha)r\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $q,r\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Utility Representation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

 is continuous if its upper and lower contour sets are closed: for every 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in X$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\{x\in X:x\succeq y\}\quad\text{and}\quad\{x\in X:y\succeq x\}
\]

\end_inset

are closed in 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
 Equivalently, if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\to x$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\succeq y$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succeq x^{n}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $y\succeq x$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Lemma
Every total preorder on a countable set has a real-valued utility representation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 be the symmetric part of the preorder.
 The quotient 
\begin_inset Formula $X/I$
\end_inset

 is countable, and the relation induced on it is a linear order by Appendix A.
 Every countable linear order admits an order embedding into 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Q}$
\end_inset

: enumerate its elements and insert the image of each new element at a rational number between the images of its already embedded lower and upper neighbors.
 Composing this embedding with the quotient map gives 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{Q}\subseteq\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\succeq y\iff u(x)\geq u(y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(Continuous utility representation) If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is a continuous total preorder on 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

, then there exists a continuous function 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 such that, for every 
\begin_inset Formula $x,y\in X$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x\succeq y\iff u(x)\geq u(y).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
This is Debreu's continuous-utility theorem specialized to Euclidean commodity space.
 The substantive topological input is that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}^{L}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is second countable and connected.
 Continuity of the preorder and the gap lemma provide a countable order-dense family of indifference classes.
 Mapping those classes into a countable dense subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and extending across the gaps yields an order-preserving continuous map.
 The result cannot be obtained by taking arbitrary limits of a utility defined only on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Q}^{L}$
\end_inset

: such limits need not exist or be independent of the approximating sequence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The theorem guarantees existence, not uniqueness.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 represents 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing on the range of 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $f\circ u$
\end_inset

 represents the same preference relation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Utility Maximization Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous and 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The utility maximization problem (UMP) is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{x\geq0}u(x)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s.t.\quad p\cdot x\leq w.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively we can define the restriction as 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:p\cdot x\leq w\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 then the UMP has a solution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Check 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is compact,
 in this case it is equivalent to proving that it is closed and bounded.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is bounded because 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}\leq w/p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

.
 It is closed because 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}$
\end_inset

,
 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{n}\leq w$
\end_inset

 and if 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

 it must be the case that 
\begin_inset Formula $lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}p\cdot x^{n}\leq lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}w$
\end_inset

 implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Weierstrass maximum theorem therefore gives a solution to the UMP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now prove further properties of the demand correspondence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The Marshallian demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\equiv arg\max_{y}\{u(y)|y\in B(p,w)\}$
\end_inset

 is non-empty,
 compact valued and upper hemi-continuous.
 Moreover,
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)=u(x)$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 (i.e.,
 the indirect utility) is continuous.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset

The result follows from Berge's maximum theorem once we verify that the correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous correspondence.
 We already know that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is continuous and that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is a compact set,
 thus making the 
\begin_inset Formula $B(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 correspondence compact valued.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{n}=(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 now assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{n}\rightarrow z$
\end_inset

,
 now we want to prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $B(z^{n})\rightarrow B(z)$
\end_inset

 where convergence is understood in the usual set-convergence sense.
 Recall that,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p^{n},w^{n})=\{x\in X|p^{n}\cdot x\leq w^{n}\}$
\end_inset

.
 First assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\in B(z^{n})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

 . We prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(z)$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\in B(z^{n})$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\cdot x^{n}\leq w^{n}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 applying the limits over this inequality we conclude that 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}\rightarrow x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{n},w^{n})\rightarrow(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(z)$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $z=(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the reverse inclusion, let 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p,w)$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x<w$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $x\in B(p^{n},w^{n})$
\end_inset

 for all sufficiently large 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

. If 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$
\end_inset

, choose 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}=(1-\varepsilon_{n})x$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon_{n}\downarrow0$
\end_inset

 slowly enough that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\cdot x^{n}\leq w^{n}$
\end_inset

. Hence the budget correspondence is continuous on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$
\end_inset

, and Berge's theorem gives the stated properties.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We need some definitions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Local nonsatiation.
 A preference relation \begin_inset Formula $\succeq$\end_inset
 is locally nonsatiated if, for every \begin_inset Formula $x\in X$\end_inset
 and every \begin_inset Formula $\varepsilon>0$\end_inset
,
 there is \begin_inset Formula $y\in X$\end_inset
 with \begin_inset Formula $\lVert y-x\rVert<\varepsilon$\end_inset
 and \begin_inset Formula $y\succ x$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If a locally nonsatiated utility function generates the Marshallian demand correspondence, then every demanded bundle exhausts the budget: \begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x=w$\end_inset
 for every \begin_inset Formula $x\in x(p,w)$\end_inset
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof.  If \begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x<w$\end_inset
,
 local nonsatiation supplies a strictly preferred bundle arbitrarily close to \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
.
 For a sufficiently small neighborhood the bundle remains affordable, contradicting utility maximization.  Therefore the budget constraint binds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The same demand correspondence is homogeneous of degree zero.  If preferences are convex, demand is convex-valued; if preferences are strictly convex, demand is single-valued.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
KKT Conditions for the UMP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Under differentiability,
 the first-order conditions characterize an interior solution.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We write the KKT
\series bold
 FOC
\series default
 necessary conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{L}=u(x)+\lambda(w-p\cdot x)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{x_{l}}u(x^{*})\leq\lambda^{*}p_{l}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{l}^{*}[\partial_{x_{l}}u(x^{*})-\lambda^{*}p_{l}]=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l\in\{1,\cdots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $w-p\cdot x^{*}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{*}(w-p\cdot x^{*})=0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a system with 
\begin_inset Formula $L+1$
\end_inset

 unknowns.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Local nonsatiation implies that the consumer is on the budget hyperplane (the frontier).
 This means that 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w-p\cdot x^{*}=0.
\]

\end_inset

For an interior solution (all quantities positive),
 the KKT conditions reduce to 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial_{x_{l}}u(x^{*})}{\partial_{x_{k}}u(x^{*})}=\frac{p_{l}}{p_{k}}\quad\forall l,k\in\{1,\cdots,L\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that for 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{p_{1}}{p_{2}}$
\end_inset

 is the slope of the budget line and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{\partial_{x_{1}}u(x^{*})}{\partial_{x_{2}}u(x^{*})}$
\end_inset

 is the slope of the indifference curve,
 usually this is also referred as the MRS.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Properties of the Indirect Utility Function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The indirect utility is the maximal utility achieved given the budget constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)=\max_{x\geq0}u(x)$
\end_inset

 s.t.
 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x\leq w$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v:P\times W\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 is a form of ranking prices and income (budgets).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively it is the utility evaluated in the Marshallian demand 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is a continuous utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 defined on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset
.
 The indirect utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
1.
 Homogeneity of degree zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
2.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

 and non-increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
3.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is quasiconvex in 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $\{(p,w)|v(p,w)\leq\overline{v}\}$
\end_inset

 is convex.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
4.
 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Proof.  Existence, homogeneity, monotonicity, and continuity follow from the compact budget correspondence and Berge's maximum theorem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For quasiconvexity, fix \begin_inset Formula $\overline v$\end_inset
 and let \begin_inset Formula $L_{\overline v}=\{(p,w):v(p,w)\leq\overline v\}$\end_inset
.
 If \begin_inset Formula $u(x)>\overline v$\end_inset
,
 then every \begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in L_{\overline v}$\end_inset
 must satisfy \begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x>w$\end_inset
,
 because otherwise \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
 would be affordable and the value would exceed \begin_inset Formula $\overline v$\end_inset
.
 Conversely, this strict inequality for every \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
 with \begin_inset Formula $u(x)>\overline v$\end_inset
 rules out an affordable bundle with utility above \begin_inset Formula $\overline v$\end_inset
.
 Hence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{\overline v}=\bigcap_{u(x)>\overline v}\{(p,w):p\cdot x>w\}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each set in the intersection is a linear half-space in \begin_inset Formula $(p,w)$\end_inset
,
 so the lower contour set is convex and \begin_inset Formula $v$\end_inset
 is quasiconvex.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Roy's Identity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall the definition of indirect utility 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,w)=u(x(p,w))$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume the maximizer is unique and the value function is differentiable.  The envelope theorem applied to
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(p,w)=\max_{x\geq0,\;p\cdot x\leq w}u(x)
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
gives
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\partial_w v(p,w)=\lambda(p,w),\qquad \nabla_p v(p,w)=-\lambda(p,w)x(p,w).
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This provides the Roy's identity (this result only works under differentiability and uniqueness)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,w)=-\frac{\nabla_{p}v(p,w)}{\partial_{w}v(p,w)}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
Quasilinear demand and Roy's identity.
 Let good \begin_inset Formula $L$\end_inset
 be the numeraire, set \begin_inset Formula $p_L=1$\end_inset
,
 and let utility be \begin_inset Formula $u(q,q_L)=g(q)+q_L$\end_inset
 for \begin_inset Formula $q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L-1}$\end_inset
.
 The indirect utility is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(p,w)=w+\max_{q\geq0}\{g(q)-p_{-L}\cdot q\}.
\]
\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When the maximizer is unique and the envelope theorem applies, Roy's identity gives \begin_inset Formula $x_k(p,w)=-\partial_{p_k}v(p,w)$\end_inset
 for \begin_inset Formula $k<L$\end_inset
,
 and the numeraire demand is the residual \begin_inset Formula $x_L=w-p_{-L}\cdot x_{-L}(p)$\end_inset
.
 This example has no income effect on the non-numeraire demands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Expenditure Minimization Problem (EMP)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Expenditure Minimization Problem (EMP) asks the question,
 if prices are 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

,
 what is the minimum amount the consumer would have to spend to achieve utility level 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{u}$
\end_inset

.
 That is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\min_{x\geq0}p\cdot x
\]

\end_inset

 s.t.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x)\geq\overline{u}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a continuous utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 defined on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and the price vector is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\gg0$
\end_inset

.
 We have.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(i) If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the UMP when 
\begin_inset Formula $w>0$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the EMP when the required level of utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 the minimized expenditure level 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}=w$
\end_inset

 is exactly the wealth 
\begin_inset Formula $w$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(ii) If 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the EMP when the required level of utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u>u(0)$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is optimal in the UMP when wealth is 
\begin_inset Formula $p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Moreover,
 the maximized utility level 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{*})=u$
\end_inset

 is exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We omit the proof.
 For a complete proof, see the Afriat treatment in the references.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Expenditure function
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $e:P\times\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R})$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,u)=p\cdot x^{*}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an element in the solution to the EMP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is a continuous utility function representing a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 defined on the consumption set 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset
, with 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}$
\end_inset
 and the target utility in the attainable range.
 The expenditure 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,u)$
\end_inset

 is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(i) Homogeneous of degree one in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula $\min_{x\geq0}\alpha p\cdot x$
\end_inset

 s.t.
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\geq\overline{u}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(ii) Strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and non-decreasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{l}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(iii) Concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(iv) Continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $p,u$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator parbreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i),(ii) are omitted.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iii) Concavity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We want to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,u)$
\end_inset

 is concave in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})\geq\alpha e(p,\overline{u})+(1-\alpha)e(p',\overline{u}),$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
when 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{\alpha}=\alpha p+(1-\alpha)p'$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 be the minimizer of the EMP,
 we abuse notation and let it denote any element of the set of optimizers at the given prices:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})=p^{\alpha}\cdot h(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $=\alpha p\cdot h(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})+(1-\alpha)(p')\cdot h(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})=p\cdot h(p,\overline{u})\leq p\cdot h(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})$
\end_inset

,
 and we can conclude that:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e(p^{\alpha},\overline{u})\ge\alpha p\cdot h(p,\overline{u})+(1-\alpha)(p')\cdot h(p',\overline{u}).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iv) Continuity in 
\begin_inset Formula $p,u$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Continuity follows from the standard expenditure-function theorem.
 The unbounded feasible set cannot be inserted directly into Berge's theorem.
 Instead, around any 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,\overline u)$
\end_inset
 with strictly positive prices, one first obtains a common expenditure upper bound from one feasible bundle.
 Strict positivity of nearby prices then confines every optimizer to a common compact box.
 Berge's theorem applies to this locally compactified problem.
 Continuity of preferences and local nonsatiation ensure feasibility stability and that an optimum reaches the target utility.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Hicksian Compensated Demand
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Hicksian Compensated Demand or Hicksian demand is 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,\overline{u})=arg\min_{x\geq0,u(x)\geq\overline{u}}p\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $u(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is continuous and it represents a locally nonsatiated preference relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

.
 Then the Hicksian demand correspondence 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,u)$
\end_inset

 has the following properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(i) HD0 in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(ii) No excess utility:
 For 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{*}\in h(p,u)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{*})=\overline{u}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(iii) Convexity/Uniqueness:
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is convex,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,u)$
\end_inset

 is a convex set,
 and if 
\begin_inset Formula $\succeq$
\end_inset

 is strictly convex,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,u)$
\end_inset

 is a unique element.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
KKT Conditions for the EMP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is twice continuously differentiable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Lagrangian for this problem is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}_{EMP}=p\cdot x-\lambda(u(x)-\overline{u}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The FOC are given by
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p\geq\lambda\nabla_{x}u(x^{*})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{l}^{*}[p_{l}-\lambda\nabla u(x^{*})_{l}]=0,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\geq0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Sheppard's Lemma
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
Every Hicksian bundle is a supergradient of the concave expenditure function in prices.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\cdot,\overline u)$
\end_inset
 is differentiable at 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset
, Hicksian demand is unique there and Shepard's identity holds:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{p}e(p,\overline{u})=h(p,\overline{u}).
\]

\end_inset
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At a differentiability point, apply the envelope theorem to the constrained problem.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
e(p,\overline{u})=p\cdot h+\lambda^{*}(u(h)-\overline{u})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We derive with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and we obtain:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\nabla_{p}e(p,\overline{u})=h(p,\overline{u})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Additional Properties
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume throughout this subsection that 
\begin_inset Formula $e(\cdot,u)$
\end_inset
 is twice continuously differentiable, so Hicksian demand is single-valued and differentiable.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)$
\end_inset

 (Jacobian) 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)_{k,l}=\partial_{p_{l}}h_{k}(p,u)$
\end_inset

 be the 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 matrix of derivatives of prices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
The Hicksian demand function has the following properties:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)=\mathcal{H}e(p,u)$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{H}e(p,u)_{k,l}=\partial_{p_{k}}\partial_{p_{l}}e(p,u)$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The derivative with respect to prices of the Hicksian demand is the Hessian of the expenditure function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a NSD matrix.
 This follows from the fact that a Hessian of a concave function is NSD.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
3.
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)$
\end_inset

 is symmetric.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Young's theorem (continuity of the second derivatives of 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial_{p_{k}}\partial_{p_{l}}e(p,u)=\partial_{p_{l}}\partial_{p_{k}}e(p,u)$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
4.
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}h(p,u)p=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This follows from HD0 of 
\begin_inset Formula $h(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 and Euler's identity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
NSD (negative semidefinite matrix) For any vector 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
 a symmetric matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 of size 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times L$
\end_inset

 is NSD if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{\top}Av\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Separator plain
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The following matrix is NSD:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\left[\begin{array}{cc}
-2 & 0\\
0 & 0
\end{array}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v'Av=-2v_{1}^{2}\leq0
\]

\end_inset

 for any 
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary of Relationships between the EMP and UMP
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\series bold
expenditure function 
\series default
is the minimum 
\begin_inset Formula $e(p,\overline{u})=p\cdot h(p,\overline{u})$
\end_inset

 value attained at the given prices and target utility.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a 
\series bold
duality relation
\series default
 between the EMP and the UMP that we will not explore at this point.
 However,
 we will state the following relations.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(p,v(p,w))=x(p,w)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p,e(p,u))=h(p,u).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\overline{u}=v(p,e(p,\overline{u}))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w=e(p,v(p,w)).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Slutsky Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Slutsky matrix is 
\begin_inset Formula $S:P\times W\to\mathbb{R}^{L\times L}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)_{l,k}=\partial_{p_{k}}x_{l}(p,w)+\partial_{w}x_{l}(p,w)x_{k}(p,w)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider price compensation that keeps the original bundle 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula $D_{p}[x(p,p\cdot q)]=D_{p}x(p,p\cdot q)+D_{w}x(p,p\cdot q)q^{\top}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q=x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for the original price and income and then replace to obtain the Slutsky matrix (under Walras' law):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}x(p,p\cdot q)_{|q=x(p,w)}=S(p,w).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We establish now a very useful equation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(p,u)=x(p,e(p,u))
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Differentiating with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}h(p,u)=D_{p}x(p,e(p,u))+D_{w}x(p,e(p,u))\nabla_{p}e(p,u)^{\top}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By Sheppard's lemma and by the identities that relate the expenditure and wealth
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
D_{p}h(p,v(p,w))=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}=S(p,w).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Summary of the Implications of Utility Maximization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If a demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is generated by maximizing a complete,
 transitive,
 continuous and locally nonsatiated preference relation represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 subject to a linear budget constraint (that is,
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)\in\arg\max_{y\in B(p,w)}u(y)$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(p,w)\in P\times W$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $u:X\to\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

.
 Then:
 (i) 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 satisfies Walras' law,
 (ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $x(p,w)$
\end_inset

 is homogeneous of degree zero.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
If, in addition, \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
 is continuously differentiable and the expenditure function is twice differentiable so that the Slutsky matrix is defined,
 its Slutsky matrix function 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)$
\end_inset

 has a negative-semidefinite symmetric part:
 (i) 
\begin_inset Formula $a^{\mathsf T}S(p,w)a\leq0$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $a\in\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 (ii) Singular in prices 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)p=0$
\end_inset

,
 (iii) Symmetric 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=S(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p,w\in P\times W$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This proposition summarizes our previous results.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Integrability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now ask for sufficient conditions under which an observed demand function is generated by utility maximization.
 In the abstract choice setting,
 WARP and GARP restrict rationalizability.
 Competitive budget sets are a smaller domain than arbitrary menus,
 so differentiable demand requires additional restrictions.
 The relevant restrictions are stated in terms of the Slutsky matrix.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of course,
 we assume in this subsection that the demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is continuously differentiable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Recall that the Slutsky matrix function is 
\begin_inset Formula $S(p,w)=D_{p}x(p,w)+D_{w}x(p,w)x(p,w)^{\top}$
\end_inset

,
 or in the scalar version 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{ij}(p,w)=\partial_{p_{j}}x_{i}(p,w)+\partial_{w}x_{i}(p,w)x_{j}(p,w)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i,j\in\{1,\cdots,L\}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem
(Local integrability theorem, cited.)  Let \begin_inset Formula $D\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{++}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{++}$\end_inset
 be an open, simply connected domain, and let \begin_inset Formula $x:D\to\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$\end_inset
 be continuously differentiable.  Suppose that \begin_inset Formula $x$\end_inset
 satisfies Walras' law and homogeneity of degree zero, and that its Slutsky matrix is symmetric and negative semidefinite on \begin_inset Formula $D$\end_inset
.
 Under the standard nondegeneracy and boundary conditions ensuring that the associated differential system has a unique solution through each point, these conditions are locally sufficient for a locally nonsatiated utility representation.  A global representation requires the corresponding global coverage and boundary conditions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The qualifications are essential.  Symmetry and negative semidefiniteness alone, stated on an unspecified domain, do not guarantee a global representation: one must control the domain, boundary behavior, and the differential equation's existence and uniqueness.  Conversely, every continuously differentiable demand generated by a regular locally nonsatiated utility satisfies Walras' law, homogeneity, symmetry, and negative semidefiniteness of the Slutsky matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark*
Some textbooks require a boundedness condition on the elasticity of wealth,
 this is in general not required.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Examples and Applications of Demand Integrability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two commodities 
\begin_inset Formula $L=2$
\end_inset

 and you estimate a demand function 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}(p,w)=\frac{\alpha w}{p_{1}}$
\end_inset

,
 and you know that the demand of good two is given as a residual.
 Furthermore,
 we have checked that this demand has a Slutsky matrix that is symmetric,
 NSD and singular in prices,
 we normalize 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=1$
\end_inset

.
 Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}=(1-\alpha)w.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\begin_inset Formula $L-1$
\end_inset

 differential equation is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu'(p_{1},1)=\frac{\alpha\mu(p_{1},1)}{p_{1}}=x_{1}((p_{1},1),\mu((p_{1},1))),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for short,
 we can write:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu'(p_{1})=\frac{\alpha\mu(p_{1})}{p_{1}}=x_{1}(p_{1},\mu(p_{1})),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can write more simply:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\mu'}{\mu}=\frac{\alpha}{p_{1}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Simple integration provides a family of solutions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
ln\mu=\alpha lnp_{1}+C,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and in levels:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu=Kp_{1}^{\alpha},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with 
\begin_inset Formula $K=\exp(C)$
\end_inset

 a constant of integration.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given some initial conditions 
\begin_inset Formula $(p_{1}^{0},w^{0})$
\end_inset

,
 we must have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w^{0}=K(p_{1}^{0})^{\alpha},\quad K=\frac{w^{0}}{(p_{1}^{0})^{\alpha}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mu(p_{1}^{*};p_{1}^{0},w^{0})=\frac{w^{0}}{(p_{1}^{0})^{\alpha}}(p_{1}^{*})^{\alpha}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now let's define the indirect utility,
 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}^{*}=1$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v(p_{1},w)=\mu(p^{*},p_{1},w)=\frac{w}{p_{1}^{\alpha}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To recover the utility 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

,
 we need to invert the demand,
 note that our primitive is indeed invertible.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We have to use the demand function and obtain the inverse demand:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{1}(p,w)=\frac{\alpha w}{p_{1}};\quad x_{2}(p,w)=(1-\alpha)w
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{x_{1}}{x_{2}}=\frac{\alpha}{(1-\alpha)}\frac{1}{p_{1}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}=\frac{\alpha}{1-\alpha}\frac{x_{2}}{x_{1}},\quad w=\frac{x_{2}}{1-\alpha}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Replacing back in the indirect utility we have:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x_{1},x_{2})=v\circ(p_{1},w)(x_{1},x_{2})=v(\frac{\alpha}{1-\alpha}\frac{x_{2}}{x_{1}},\frac{x_{2}}{1-\alpha}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x_{1},x_{2})=\frac{\frac{x_{2}}{1-\alpha}}{\left(\frac{\alpha}{1-\alpha}\frac{x_{2}}{x_{1}}\right)^{\alpha}}=\left(\frac{x_{2}}{1-\alpha}\right)^{1-\alpha}\left(\frac{x_{1}}{\alpha}\right)^{\alpha}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u(x_{1},x_{2})=\hat{c}x_{1}^{\alpha}x_{2}^{1-\alpha},\quad\hat{c}=(1-\alpha)^{\alpha}\alpha^{\alpha}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is a Cobb-Douglas utility function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Generalized Axiom of Revealed Preference (GARP)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now move from unrestricted demand functions to finite observations.
 Suppose that the dataset is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{p^{k},x^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $K\geq2$
\end_inset

.
 Walras' law is maintained throughout this section.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}$
\end_inset

 is directly revealed preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}R^{D}x)$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\cdot x^{t}\geq p^{t}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Furthermore,
 we define an (indirect) notion of revealed preference.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
We say that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}$
\end_inset

 is (indirectly) revealed preferred to 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{t}Rx)$
\end_inset

 if and only if there is a sequence of commodities such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}R^{D}x^{1}R^{D}x^{2}R^{D}\cdots x^{n}R^{D}x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 Using some ideas from order theory we can see that 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is the transitive closure of 
\begin_inset Formula $R^{D}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the previous treatment of rationality we did not allow for correspondences;
 we only allowed for functions.
 But what happens for cases when we observe for the same prices 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}=p^{s}$
\end_inset

 different demand choices 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}\neq x^{s}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\cdot x^{t}=p^{s}\cdot x^{s}$
\end_inset

,
 recall that this is a case where from the same budget set two different choice bundles are chosen (this can be seen as the case of a correspondence generating the data).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
We say that a utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 (weakly) rationalizes a dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}$
\end_inset

,
 whenever for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t\in\{1,\cdots,K\}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})\geq u(x)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\cdot x^{t}\geq p^{t}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We now introduce a property that serves as a test of rationalizability.
 We can define the strict direct preference relation as 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{t}P^{D}x^{s}$
\end_inset

 if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\cdot x^{t}>p^{t}\cdot x^{s}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A finite demand dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{(p^{t},x^{t})\}_{t=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 satisfies the generalized axiom of revealed preference (GARP) if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{t}Rx^{s}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad\neg\bigl(x^{s}P^{D}x^{t}\bigr)
\]

\end_inset

for all observations 
\begin_inset Formula $s,t$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is the transitive closure of direct revealed preference and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x^{s}P^{D}x^{t}\iff p^{s}\cdot x^{s}>p^{s}\cdot x^{t}.
\]

\end_inset

Equivalently, a chain of weak revealed-preference comparisons may not be closed by a strict direct comparison in the opposite direction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
(Afriat's Theorem) Given 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{p^{k},x^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

,
 the following are equivalent:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
1.
 There exists a locally nonsatiated utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 that rationalizes the data in the sense that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{k})\geq u(x)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{k}\cdot x^{k}\geq p^{k}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
2.
 The dataset 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}=\{p^{k},x^{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}$
\end_inset

 satisfies GARP.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
3.
 There exist numbers 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{t}\in\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{t}>0$
\end_inset

 to the set of linear inequalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{t}\leq u^{s}+\lambda^{s}p^{s}\cdot (x^{t}-x^{s})\forall s,t\in\{1,\cdots,K\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition
4.
 There exists a continuous,
 concave,
 and strictly monotonic,
 utility function 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 that rationalizes the data in the sense that for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{k})\geq u(x)$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{k}\cdot x^{k}\geq p^{k}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For a differentiable rationalization, (4) implies (3) through supporting first-order inequalities; at a boundary coordinate, the KKT inequality is multiplied by a nonnegative observed quantity.
 For a nonsmooth concave rationalization, the same argument uses a positive supergradient at each observation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is concave then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})\leq u(x^{s})+\nabla u(x^{s})\cdot(x^{t}-x^{s})$
\end_inset

.
 Given that 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is assumed smooth 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u$
\end_inset

 exists (super-gradient for concave functions).
 Now,
 by the KKT conditions for the UMP, 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla u(x^{s})\leq\lambda^{s}p^{s}$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})\leq u(x^{s})+\lambda^{s}p^{s}\cdot (x^{t}-x^{s})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})=u^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{proof}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(1)\implies(2),(2)\implies(3),(3)\implies(4),(4)\implies(1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

) implies (2),
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{O}$
\end_inset

 is rationalized by a nonsatiated utility function and that GARP fails,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{1}Rx^{2}\cdots Rx^{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}P^{D}x^{1}$
\end_inset

 with strict preference for the last one,
 this means that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{1})\geq u(x^{2})\geq\cdots\geq u(x^{n})$
\end_inset

 and also that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\cdot x^{n}>p^{n}\cdot x^{1}$
\end_inset

 but this means that there is a 
\begin_inset Formula $||y-x^{1}||<\epsilon$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{n}\cdot x^{n}>p^{n}\cdot y$
\end_inset

 and such that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y)>u(x^{1})$
\end_inset

 by local nonsatiation.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(y)>u(x^{n})$
\end_inset

 which means that 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{n}$
\end_inset

 cannot be a maximizer for budget 
\begin_inset Formula $B(p^{n},p^{n}\cdot x^{n})$
\end_inset

,
 this is a contradiction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(2)$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $(3)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The implication from GARP to the Afriat inequalities is the finite Afriat feasibility lemma.
 Write the inequalities as a finite linear feasibility problem in 
\begin_inset Formula $(u^{1},\ldots,u^{K},\lambda^{1},\ldots,\lambda^{K})$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{t}>0$
\end_inset

.
 Farkas' lemma says that failure of feasibility is equivalent to a nonnegative dual certificate.
 After collecting the price-expenditure terms along the directed revealed-preference graph, such a certificate is exactly a revealed-preference cycle with at least one strict reversal.
 GARP excludes every such cycle, so the system is feasible.
 Conversely, summing the Afriat inequalities around a revealed-preference cycle rules out a strict reversal.
 This gives the equivalence between statements (2) and (3).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(3)$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $(4)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\min_{t\in\{1,\cdots,K\}}\{u^{t}+\lambda^{t}p^{t}\cdot (x-x^{t})\}$
\end_inset

,
 this function is continuous in 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 and concave,
 it is also strictly increasing in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 (given that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda^{t}>0$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{t}\gg0$
\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To verify rationalization, first observe that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{t})=u^{t}$
\end_inset

 for every observation 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Indeed, the Afriat inequality gives
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u^{t}\leq u^{m}+\lambda^{m}p^{m}\cdot(x^{t}-x^{m})
\]

\end_inset

for every 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

, while the term with 
\begin_inset Formula $m=t$
\end_inset

 equals 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore the minimum of these affine terms is exactly 
\begin_inset Formula $u^{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It remains to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)\leq u(x^{s})$
\end_inset

,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{s}\cdot x^{s}\geq p^{s}\cdot x$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{s}\cdot x^{s}\geq p^{s}\cdot x$
\end_inset

,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{s}\cdot (x^{s}-x)\geq0$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x)=\min_{t}\{u^{t}+\lambda^{t}p^{t}\cdot (x-x^{t})\}\leq u^{s}+\lambda^{s}p^{s}\cdot (x-x^{s})\leq u^{s}=u(x^{s})$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $u(x^{s})\geq u(x)$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{s}\cdot x^{s}\geq p^{s}\cdot x$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(4)$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $(1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 is strictly monotonic then it is locally nonsatiated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
 
\end_layout

